Subject: dropbear on Android: /data/local/dropbear, /system/bin/sh, client key directory, getpass()

Why: Android has no home directories for its users (bionic's getpwnam() gives "/" or /data), no
/etc/passwd shells, no /etc/shells and no getpass() in bionic, and /data is owned by system (uid 1000),
so upstream's checks of ~/.ssh/authorized_keys and of the login shell reject every login.
- svr-runopts.c: the default authorized_keys directory (-D) is /data/local/dropbear for every user.
- svr-authpubkey.c: check the authorized_keys file and its directory (owned by the user or root, not
  group/other writable), not every path component up to "/".
- common-session.c, svr-auth.c: the login shell is /system/bin/sh and is accepted without /etc/shells.
- dbutil.c, cli-kex.c, cli-runopts.c: dbclient keeps known_hosts and the default identity id_dropbear in
  $HOME/.ssh, or in /data/local/dropbear when HOME is unset, "/" or not writable.
- dbutil.c: a getpass() that reads /dev/tty with echo off (dbclient password/keyboard-interactive auth).
Source: written for this module. Termux's dropbear patches (termux-packages packages/dropbear,
common-session.c.patch and svr-auth.c.patch at commit 4e132cc744b8f8996f647ac9e6318f94ae1393e6) force the
login shell and skip the /etc/shells check in the same way for Termux's single-user server; Termux keeps
upstream's ~/.ssh paths and uses termux-auth instead of getpass()/crypt().

